  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "## Summary\n\nValtuutus 3.0.2 closes the remaining gap in reading your own writes without a snap token, which 3.0.1 restored only in part. **Postgres and SQL Server need a migration**; MySQL and MariaDB upgrade by bumping the packages.\n\n## Fixes\n\n- **A request without a snap token keeps seeing a write once that write returned (#393). Still broken in 3.0.1.**\n  - In 3.0.1, a write could return (or  could return) and a request without a token could still miss it moments later. A concurrent write that took an older id, but registered as in flight only after the newer write had returned, pulled the latest settled token back below the newer write until it committed. It showed up as ,  or a lookup right after a write still answering with the old state, intermittently and only under concurrent writes.\n  - Now the latest settled token never moves back below a write that already returned. A write that took an older id commits above every write that committed before it registered, so readers no longer stop below those writes while it is open.\n  - Applies to every relational provider. InMemory was never affected.\n  - **Cost** (measured against 3.0.1 with concurrent writers and readers): Postgres and MySQL within noise. SQL Server writes within noise; requests without a token are 2.5% slower with 4 concurrent writers and 9% slower with 16.\n\n## Migrations\n\nRun the new scripts before deploying (or let the Weasel package apply them):\n\n- Postgres: [](src/Valtuutus.Data.Postgres/Database/migrations/20261110120000_transactions_settled.sql) adds  and a partial index on it. On a large  table, consider creating the index  outside a transaction.\n- SQL Server: [](src/Valtuutus.Data.SqlServer/Database/migrations/20261110120000_transactions_pending_newest_committed.sql) adds a nullable column to .\n- MySQL and MariaDB: none.\n\nNodes still on 3.0.1 keep working against the migrated schema during a rolling deploy; the fix takes effect once every node runs 3.0.2.",
